<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk.WebAssembly">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0-browser</TargetFramework>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <AllowUnsafeBlocks>true</AllowUnsafeBlocks>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <!--
      Relink the runtime natively. Ava3D calls GL entry points whose signatures are not in the
      prebuilt WebAssembly trampoline table — glClearColor's four floats, for one — and calling those
      aborts the runtime with no exception and no message. Relinking generates the missing trampolines
      from the app's own P/Invoke set. Costs about a minute of build time and needs the wasm-tools
      workload; without it the browser build dies on the first frame.
    -->
    <WasmBuildNative>true</WasmBuildNative>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Avalonia.Browser" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      The five assemblies that reach native code through a function pointer, kept whole through the
      trimmer. Without this the application publishes cleanly and then draws nothing.

      GlEntryPoints binds every GL function this renderer needs beyond Avalonia's own set as an
      [UnmanagedFunctionPointer] delegate handed to Marshal.GetDelegateForFunctionPointer, and Avalonia
      and SkiaSharp reach their own native layers the same way. Calling one in WebAssembly needs a
      trampoline for that exact signature, and WasmBuildNative generates those by scanning the
      application for the signatures it uses. Nothing references those delegate types statically — they
      are reached through a generic — so the trimmer takes them, the scan then finds no signatures to
      build, and the first GL call aborts the runtime at aot-runtime-wasm.c:188 with an ExitStatus
      carrying no message. The picture is black, no exception escapes, and every diagnostic the
      application prints about itself says nothing is wrong. That is the failure that kept this demo on
      an untrimmed build for three releases.

      The CPU renderer survives trimming without any of this, because it never calls a GL entry point —
      which is why the fault looked like "publishing is broken" rather than "the GL path is".

      The set is deliberately generous rather than minimal: it costs two megabytes against a
      twenty-megabyte saving, and a narrower list would be a thing to rediscover the next time a
      dependency moves. What it buys is the trimmed build, which is the only way to get
      RunAOTCompilation — and that is worth ten times the frame rate on the CPU renderer.
    -->
    <TrimmerRootAssembly Include="Ava3D" />
    <TrimmerRootAssembly Include="Avalonia.OpenGL" />
    <TrimmerRootAssembly Include="Avalonia.Base" />
    <TrimmerRootAssembly Include="Avalonia.Skia" />
    <TrimmerRootAssembly Include="SkiaSharp" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Ava3D.Demo\Ava3D.Demo.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
